\nWe can see that $C$ has $3$ pivots in the $1$st, $3$rd, and $4$th columns:\n\n![](images/05/05264585d59a4430f5424b91f649eecc80a5f28fa460a935f5c224882e08d56c.svg)\n\nTherefore, the only non-pivot column is the $2$nd column." 13194139534217 17592186045417 true] [17592186124291 1074 "Remember that the pivots of a matrix in row echelon form are the first non-zero entries in each row of the matrix.\n\nWe can see that $C$ has $3$ pivots in the $1$st, $3$rd, and $4$th columns:\n\n![](images/da/da73e68fe8bc5e6baa8876beb17d702707cb0ad43831f42ecfa45f3a98d43202.png)\n\nTherefore, the only non-pivot column is the $2$nd column." 13194139534217 17592186045417 false] [17592186124292 1071 "Determine the number of pivot columns of the matrix $A,$ given by\n![](images/df/dfe650778e528254fc2098714566d954c47442ede485d6a92495cf16eb9e936d.svg)" 13194139534217 17592186045417 true] [17592186124292 1071 "Determine the number of pivot columns of the matrix $A,$ given by\n![](images/eb/eb0fc447f75650bb47da114b8cd4f0986b5f501333f61005e2c1f4989c3a11ed.png)" 13194139534217 17592186045417 false] [17592186124293 1071 "What are the pivot columns of the matrix $B,$ given by\n![](images/b5/b57dc02f6baddfcb517ccf12366320534074b33814577b4a2d1010377325fc19.png)" 13194139534217 17592186045417 false] [17592186124293 1071 "What are the pivot columns of the matrix $B,$ given by\n![](images/f1/f10a5ef881920312ccdb163d9db70779d3848f7dc510c29d516eaf74a5ddd38d.svg)" 13194139534217 17592186045417 true] [17592186124477 1038 #edb/ref 17592186414495 13194139534217 17592186045417 true] [17592186124477 1038 #edb/ref 17592186414496 13194139534217 17592186045417 true] [17592186124478 1038 #edb/ref 17592186414497 13194139534217 17592186045417 true] [17592186124479 1038 #edb/ref 17592186414494 13194139534217 17592186045417 true] [17592186124704 1050 480.0 13194139534217 17592186045417 true] [17592186124884 1068 #edb/ref 17592186121904 13194139534217 17592186045417 false] [17592186124884 1068 #edb/ref 17592186414489 13194139534217 17592186045417 true] [17592186124884 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139534217 17592186045417 true] [17592186124884 1071 "Determine the number of pivot columns of the matrix $B$, given by\n$B = \\begin{bmatrix}3 & 0 & 0 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 2 & 0 & -3 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 10 & 3 & -9 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 & 1 & 2\\end{bmatrix}$." 13194139534217 17592186045417 false] [17592186124884 1071 "Determine the number of pivot columns of the matrix $B,$ given by\n\n$$\nB=[\\begin{aligned}3 & 0 & 0 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 2 & 0 & -3 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 10 & 3 & -9 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 & 1 & 2\\end{aligned}].\n$$" 13194139534217 17592186045417 true] [17592186124884 1074 "First, we need to convert $B$ to row echelon form:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}B & =[\\begin{aligned}3 & 0 & 0 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 2 & 0 & -3 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 10 & 3 & -9 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 & 1 & 2\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{3} & :={R}_{3}+(-5){R}_{2} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}3 & 0 & 0 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 2 & 0 & -3 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 & 3 & 6 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 & 1 & 2\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{4} & :={R}_{4}+(-\\frac{1}{3}){R}_{3} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}3 & 0 & 0 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 2 & 0 & -3 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 & 3 & 6 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 & 0 & 0\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLooking at the row echelon form, we see that $A$ has $3$ pivots, and therefore the number of pivot columns is $3.$" 13194139534217 17592186045417 true] [17592186125757 1064 "The **pivots** of a matrix in row echelon form are the first non-zero entries in each row of the matrix.\n\nFor example, given the matrix \n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & -2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 5 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nwhich is in row echelon form, let's find the pivots of $A.$\n\n- The first non-zero entry in the first row is the entry $a_{11}=1.$ So this is a pivot.\n\n- The first non-zero entry of the second row is $a_{22}=1.$ So this is also a pivot.\n\n- The first non-zero entry in the third row is $a_{34}=1.$ Therefore this is a pivot.\n\nThe pivots of $A$ are highlighted below.\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & -2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 5 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nThe **pivot columns** of a matrix in row echelon form are simply the columns in which the pivots appear.\n\nFor our matrix $A$, we can see that the pivot columns are the $1$st, $2$nd, and $4$th columns.\n\nThe non-pivot columns are the columns that do not contain a pivot. So for the matrix $A,$ the only non-pivot column is the $3$rd column." 13194139534217 17592186045417 false] [17592186125757 1064 "The **pivots** of a matrix in row echelon form are the first non-zero entries in each row of the matrix.\n\nFor example, given the matrix \n![](images/ad/addf5cd1e1219a1e1ce28f3d27d1b000b3a99640ff89a62e55b66f3a5cdb3521.svg)\nwhich is in row echelon form, let's find the pivots of $A.$\n\n- The first non-zero entry in the first row is the entry $a_{11}=1.$ So this is a pivot.\n\n- The first non-zero entry of the second row is $a_{22}=1.$ So this is also a pivot.\n\n- The first non-zero entry in the third row is $a_{34}=1.$ Therefore this is a pivot.\n\nThe pivots of $A$ are highlighted below.\n\n![](images/96/96dcf8cf9f0d387e0539ea04dd7ae4a498534361ad6bd5ed3bb119ae21f0416a.svg)\n\nThe **pivot columns** of a matrix in row echelon form are simply the columns in which the pivots appear.\n\nFor our matrix $A$, we can see that the pivot columns are the $1$st, $2$nd, and $4$th columns.\n\nThe non-pivot columns are the columns that do not contain a pivot. So for the matrix $A,$ the only non-pivot column is the $3$rd column." 13194139534217 17592186045417 true] [17592186414464 1080 "2" 13194139534217 17592186045417 true] [17592186414464 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 13194139534217 17592186045417 true] [17592186414464 1083 #uuid "0bf2fc69-40c7-50ed-b76f-0a01f02146c1" 13194139534217 17592186045417 true] [17592186414465 1080 "All the columns are pivot" 13194139534217 17592186045417 true] [17592186414465 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045499 13194139534217 17592186045417 true] [17592186414465 1083 #uui